WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.510
A pupil showed me a screenshot,

2
00:00:02.950 --> 00:00:04.070
very proud of himself.

3
00:00:04.950 --> 00:00:07.890
He had got a chatbot to write a

4
00:00:07.890 --> 00:00:10.430
cruel poem about a classmate.

5
00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:14.480
At first it refused, so he told it

6
00:00:14.480 --> 00:00:16.720
it was “just for a story”.

7
00:00:17.800 --> 00:00:19.980
Today: why chatbots can be

8
00:00:19.980 --> 00:00:21.980
talked round their rules, and

9
00:00:21.980 --> 00:00:23.340
what a school does about it.

10
00:00:24.520 --> 00:00:25.900
Chatbots are trained to

11
00:00:25.900 --> 00:00:27.720
refuse harmful requests.

12
00:00:28.560 --> 00:00:31.100
But that refusal is learned, like

13
00:00:31.100 --> 00:00:32.680
everything else the model does.

14
00:00:33.390 --> 00:00:35.630
It learned to say no to the kinds of

15
00:00:35.630 --> 00:00:36.990
wording it saw in training.

16
00:00:37.580 --> 00:00:39.760
A new wording may not trigger it.

17
00:00:40.910 --> 00:00:43.670
A jailbreak is an attempt to talk a

18
00:00:43.670 --> 00:00:45.870
chatbot round its safety rules.

19
00:00:46.580 --> 00:00:49.580
People try role play, “it's only for a

20
00:00:49.580 --> 00:00:52.120
story”, splitting a request into

21
00:00:52.120 --> 00:00:54.420
harmless-looking pieces, another

22
00:00:54.420 --> 00:00:57.360
language, or wearing it down over many

23
00:00:57.360 --> 00:00:57.980
turns.

24
00:00:59.060 --> 00:01:00.920
Why does this matter at school?

25
00:01:01.650 --> 00:01:04.110
Pupils swap tricks like these, and the

26
00:01:04.110 --> 00:01:06.370
results get screenshotted and shared.

27
00:01:07.070 --> 00:01:09.470
A chatbot on your school's website can

28
00:01:09.470 --> 00:01:11.470
be pushed into saying something harmful,

29
00:01:11.990 --> 00:01:13.570
with the school's name on it.

30
00:01:14.730 --> 00:01:15.210
Quick check!

31
00:01:15.850 --> 00:01:18.950
A chatbot refused a request, then

32
00:01:18.950 --> 00:01:21.250
answered when the same request was

33
00:01:21.250 --> 00:01:22.230
dressed up as a story.

34
00:01:22.920 --> 00:01:23.980
What does that show?

35
00:01:25.210 --> 00:01:27.170
Its refusals are learned, so they

36
00:01:27.170 --> 00:01:28.530
don't cover every wording.

37
00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:31.160
That's no reason to give up on the model.

38
00:01:31.940 --> 00:01:33.500
It's a reason not to rely

39
00:01:33.500 --> 00:01:34.540
on the model alone.

40
00:01:35.860 --> 00:01:37.440
Defenders test this on

41
00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:38.920
purpose, with permission.

42
00:01:39.610 --> 00:01:42.250
It's called red teaming: run a bank

43
00:01:42.250 --> 00:01:43.930
of requests the chatbot should

44
00:01:43.930 --> 00:01:46.910
refuse, count how many it does, and

45
00:01:46.910 --> 00:01:48.290
read the ones that got through.

46
00:01:48.920 --> 00:01:52.120
Each miss is a finding: what was tried,

47
00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:55.080
what got through, and how bad it is.

48
00:01:56.280 --> 00:01:59.220
Our small model, Darsi Tiny, refused

49
00:01:59.220 --> 00:02:01.420
six of eight test requests in

50
00:02:01.420 --> 00:02:03.700
English, and seven in Arabic.

51
00:02:04.450 --> 00:02:06.050
A rule in code caught the

52
00:02:06.050 --> 00:02:07.670
rest: eight out of eight.

53
00:02:08.900 --> 00:02:10.440
In the app, run the bank

54
00:02:10.440 --> 00:02:12.040
against Darsi Tiny.

55
00:02:12.670 --> 00:02:14.850
Read each miss: what slipped

56
00:02:14.850 --> 00:02:17.030
through, and how bad it is.

57
00:02:17.640 --> 00:02:20.180
Then add a rule in code and run it again.

58
00:02:21.390 --> 00:02:22.130
Your turn.

59
00:02:22.770 --> 00:02:26.510
My pupils want a contest: who can get the

60
00:02:26.510 --> 00:02:29.730
class chatbot to say the rudest thing.

61
00:02:30.420 --> 00:02:31.340
What do I say?

62
00:02:32.510 --> 00:02:34.530
No contests: report what

63
00:02:34.530 --> 00:02:35.590
slips through instead.

64
00:02:36.380 --> 00:02:39.640
Remember: refusals are learned, so

65
00:02:39.640 --> 00:02:41.280
new wordings can slip past.

66
00:02:42.010 --> 00:02:45.150
Don't rely on the model alone: layer

67
00:02:45.150 --> 00:02:47.990
filters, checks in code and people.

68
00:02:48.590 --> 00:02:50.770
And test on purpose, with

69
00:02:50.770 --> 00:02:52.470
permission, then fix.

70
00:02:53.850 --> 00:02:55.090
Try it in class tomorrow:

71
00:02:55.370 --> 00:02:57.130
play “The rule keeper”.

72
00:02:57.600 --> 00:02:59.400
One pupil guards a harmless

73
00:02:59.400 --> 00:03:01.880
secret, like the colour of a box,

74
00:03:02.260 --> 00:03:05.060
with one rule: never say it.

75
00:03:05.610 --> 00:03:07.730
Classmates try polite rewordings.

76
00:03:08.390 --> 00:03:11.510
Count what slips past, then add a second

77
00:03:11.510 --> 00:03:13.430
keeper who checks every answer.
